  m_text: The workpiece is clamped securely to the machine's table or in a vise.
    The cutting tool, also known as a milling cutter, is mounted on a spindle that
    rotates at high speed. The table, and sometimes the spindle, can move along multiple
    axes (typically X, Y, and Z, and optionally additional rotary axes) to position
    the workpiece relative to the rotating cutter. As the rotating cutter contacts
    the workpiece, it removes small pieces of material (chips) until the desired
    shape is achieved. Coolant fluids are often used to reduce heat, lubricate the
    tool, and flush away chips.
